ecord.co.uk/lifestyle/motoring/drivers-warned-easter-treat-can-35037181\",\"contentType\":{\"type\":\"news\",\"subType\":\"news_story\"},\"publishedDate\":\"12:01, 11 Apr 2025\",\"updatedDate\":\"12:01, 11 Apr 2025\",\"isSensitiveArticle\":false,\"hasActiveLegalProceedings\":false,\"storymeta\":\"alcohol,easter,lifestyle,motoring-news\",\"sections\":{\"pageSection\":\"lifestyle\",\"pagesecondarySection\":\"motoring\",\"pagetertiarySection\":\"\"},\"converseEnabled\":false}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"91:T1205,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"NewsArticle\",\"mainEntityOfPage\":\"https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/lifestyle/motoring/drivers-warned-easter-treat-can-35037181\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-GB\",\"headline\":\"Drivers warned of Easter treat that can trigger a false breathalyser test\",\"name\":\"Drivers warned of Easter treat that can trigger a false breathalyser test\",\"description\":\"The popular Easter snack, of which 20 million packs are sold every year, could land you in a pickle with the police\",\"articleBody\":\"Easter  is a holiday filled with great food choices. From c hocolate eggs  to roast lamb with all the trimmings there's no shortage of yummy grub to see you through. But drivers are being sounded a cautionary warning over eating one Easter staple before jumping behind the wheel of the car. The humble hot cross bun, which comes in many variations nowadays, can land you in hot water if you are pulled over and  breathalysed,  say insurance experts. And that's because the popular treat can trigger a false positive result on the breath test so you might want to abstain if you're planning on heading out in the car. Motoring experts from  Ripe Motorhome Insurance  have shared an important reminder to motorists to be aware of the lesser-known side effects of the sweet buns, which symbolise the crucifixion of  Jesus  and the rebirth of spring, on a breathalyser test. Alan Thomas, CEO of Ripe Motorhome Insurance, explained: “Just one bite of a  hot cross bun  can increase your blood alcohol level from 0 up to 0.018.” However, rest assured that consuming a couple of them before driving won't be putting you over the legal limit to drive. Thomas said: \\\"Foods that have started to  ferment , such as hot cross buns containing yeast, can create trace amounts of mouth alcohol, which may show up on a breathalyser test. \\\"While this isn’t reflective of the actual  blood alcohol level  in your system, this means that consuming a hot cross bun or two could get you into hot water if pulled over and lead to an unnecessary follow-up test which is the last thing you’d want over the Easter weekend!\\\" If you were pulled over and tested after chomping on a hot cross bun, any skewing of the test would be temporary. That's because they contain sugars which can interfere with the reading. But these sugars will quickly pass through your system and the reading will return to normal in a short period of time. This would usually happen within around ten minutes. And there's no need to panic if you find yourself in this situation. Police breathalysers normally involve a waiting period and several tests to mitigate this type of error. Hot cross buns aren't the only foods that can throw up these types of false positives on a breathalyser test. Other foods and drinks include fermented drinks such as kombucha, some energy drinks, foods like sauerkraut, kimchi and even yoghurts, which too have been through the fermentation process. Even vanilla extract, which contains 35% alcohol, could be detected by a breathalyser although consuming it would not make you intoxicated. Scotland has the lowest drink driving limits in the UK, 22 microgrammes (mcg) of alcohol in 100 ml of breath, 50 milligrammes (mg) of alcohol in 100ml of blood and 67 milligrammes (mg) in 100 ml of urine So if you're partial to a hot cross bun during the spring season there's no need to worry. The advice is to just wash it down with a glass of water before driving if you’re worried about a false-positive reading.\",\"datePublished\":\"2025-04-11T11:01:56Z\",\"dateModified\":\"2025-04-11T11:01:56Z\",\"image\":[\"https://i2-prod.dailyrecord.co.uk/article27748652.ece/ALTERNATES/s1200d/1_drinkjpeg.jpg\",\"https://i2-prod.dailyrecord.co.uk/article27748652.ece/ALTERNATES/s1200b/1_drinkjpeg.jpg\",\"https://i2-prod.dailyrecord.co.uk/article27748652.ece/ALTERNATES/s1200c/1_drinkjpeg.jpg\"],\"author\":[{\"@type\":\"Person\",\"name\":\"Sally McLean\",\"sameAs\":\"https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/authors/sally-mclean/\"}],\"publisher\":{\"@type\":\"NewsMediaOrganization\",\"name\":\"Daily Record\",\"url\":\"https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk\",\"publishingPrinciples\":\"https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/about-us/\",\"logo\":{\"@type\":\"ImageObject\",\"url\":\"https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/nav-web-static/main-0be4de3fe683999b5c7bc0a5c9ed622c4eeb7147/public/assets/dailyrecord/logos/masthead-dailyrecord-default.svg\",\"width\":396,\"height\":60},\"sameAs\":[\"https://www.facebook.com/TheScottishDailyRecord\",\"https://x.com/Daily_Record\",\"https://www.instagram.com/scottishdailyrecord\"]},\"speakable\":{\"@type\":\"SpeakableSpecification\",\"cssSelector\":[\"#article-body h2[data-testid='leadtext']\"]}}"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"88:[[],[[\"$\",\"script\",\"jsonld-0\",{\"type\":\"application/ld+json\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"$91\"}}],\"$L92\"]]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"89:[[\"$\",\"$L7c\",null,{\"moduleIds\":[\"app/article/[...article]/page.tsx -\u003e @/components/organisms/SlideViewer/SlideViewerWrapper\"]}],[\"$\",\"$L93\",null,{\"adRepeatEvery\":3,\"imageViewer\":\"$undefined\",\"images\":[{\"imageId\":\"27748652\",\"fileName\":\"1_drinkjpeg.jpg\",\"name\":\"image\",\"publication\":{\"name\":\"dailyrecord\",\"platform\":\"nationals\"},\"data\":{\"aspect\":1.48,\"crop\":\"default\",\"captionTitle\":\"A popular Easter treat could trigger a